  "query_preview": "(This evening through Monday)...\nIssued at 1253 PM EDT Mon Sep 14 2026\n\nOverview.\n\nHot and humid conditions return on Tuesday as high pressure slides \noff to the east and warm air streams back into Central Indiana. A \nstalled weather front over the region will bring periodic chances \nfor showers and thunderstorms from Tuesday afternoon through mid to \nlate week. High temperatures will top out in the lower to mid 90s \nthrough Wednesday before a stronger cold front clears the area over \nthe weekend, bringing cooler and drier weather by next Monday.\n\nTonight Through Wednesday.\n\nSurface high pressure drifting eastward into New England will allow \nlow-level return flow to intensify overnight, initiating theta-e \nadvection across the Mississippi and Ohio Valleys. Guidance from the \nHRRR and HREF indicates an overnight 850mb LLJ of 35-40 kt \nsupporting upstream convection over the Upper Midwest. Remnant \nconvective debris and weakening showers may reach northern portions \nof the forecast area Tuesday morning before the LLJ decays. Diurnal \nheating within strong return flow will drive Tuesday afternoon \nmaximum temperatures into the lower 90s, with surface dewpoints \nrising into the upper 60s and lower 70s. Model soundings display \nsteep low-level lapse rates below 700mb, yielding SBCAPE values \nexceeding 2000 J/kg and DCAPE above 1000 J/kg. Weak tropospheric \nshear will constrain storm organization, but pulse convection will \ncarry a localized threat for damaging microburst winds into Tuesday \nevening. Outside of thunderstorms, gradient wind gusts up to 20 mph \nare expected during the afternoon hours with the mixing down of \nboundary layer winds.\n\nThe baroclinic zone sags southward into Central Indiana Tuesday \nnight into Wednesday as a series of shortwave impulses ripple \nthrough mid-level westerly flow aloft. Substantial moisture \ntransport will push PWAT values to 1.75-2.00 inches across the warm \nsector. Strong PVA combined with low-level convergence along the \nboundary will trigger periodic convective clusters overnight Tuesday \nand through Wednesday. Given deep warm-cloud depths and mean flow \noriented parallel to the surface boundary, efficient warm-rain \nprocesses will present a risk for localized training and heavy \nrainfall. High temperatures on Wednesday will exhibit a sharp \ngradient, ranging from the upper 80s north of the boundary to the \nmid 90s south. \n\nThursday Through Monday.\n\nThe synoptic pattern through late week features strong sub-tropical \nridging anchored over the southeastern states with active zonal flow \nacross the northern tier of the CONUS. Central Indiana remains \npositioned along the northern periphery of the thermal ridge, \nkeeping the quasi-stationary surface front positioned over the \nregion. Both GFS and ECMWF deterministic solutions, along with their \nrespective GEFS and EPS ensemble means, project elevated mid-level \nmoisture and weak convective shortwaves continuing to generate \ndiurnally enhanced showers and thunderstorms Thursday and Friday. \nHigh temperatures will remain elevated in the upper 80s to lower 90s \nwith high ambient humidity. \n\nA pattern shift occurs over the weekend as a deeper upper-level \ntrough traverses the Upper Midwest and Great Lakes basin. The \nassociated surface cyclone will push the cold front southward \nthrough the Ohio Valley by Saturday night. Upper-level dynamics and \nstrengthening PVA along the trough axis will support widespread \nprecipitation along the frontal zone. Strong post-frontal CAA will \nestablish northwest flow in the 925-850mb layer by Sunday morning, \ndriving a slightly cooler airmass into the region. Radiational \ncooling beneath building surface high pressure will lower high \ntemperatures into the lower 80s by next Monday.",
